### Comprehensive Study of Thread Synchronization, Monitors & Mutex in Java

#### 1. Why Synchronization is Critical:
When multiple threads read and write to the same shared memory location concurrently without coordination, an interleaving of low-level CPU assembly operations leads to a **Race Condition**, producing corrupted, non-deterministic state.

#### 2. The Intrinsic Lock (Monitor) Mechanism:
In Java, every object has an associated **Monitor** (or Intrinsic Lock).
- A thread wishing to execute a synchronized method or block must first **acquire** the object's monitor.
- If another thread currently holds the monitor, the acquiring thread is forced into the **BLOCKED** state.
- Upon method completion (or abnormal exception), the monitor is automatically released, allowing waiting threads to contend for acquisition.

#### 3. Synchronized Method vs. Synchronized Block:
| Parameter | Synchronized Method | Synchronized Block |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Scope of Lock** | Locks the entire method body. | Locks only the critical block of code. |
| **Lock Object** | Implicitly `this` (or `ClassName.class` for static). | Can lock any arbitrary object monitor (`synchronized(lockObj)`). |
| **Performance** | Higher lock contention; holds lock during non-critical work. | Fine-grained locking; yields higher concurrent throughput. |

#### 4. Complete Practical Implementation: Bank Account Transaction System:
```java
class BankAccount {
    private int balance = 1000;

    // Synchronized method ensuring mutual exclusion
    public synchronized void withdraw(int amount, String customerName) {
        System.out.println("[" + customerName + "] Attempting to withdraw ₹" + amount);

        if (balance >= amount) {
            try {
                Thread.sleep(100); // Simulate database latency
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {}

            balance -= amount;
            System.out.println("[" + customerName + "] Withdrawal Successful! Remaining Balance: ₹" + balance);
        } else {
            System.err.println("[" + customerName + "] Insufficient Funds! Balance is only ₹" + balance);
        }
    }

    public int getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}

public class SynchronizationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        BankAccount account = new BankAccount();

        // Two customer threads trying to withdraw simultaneously
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -> account.withdraw(700, "Alice"), "Thread-Alice");
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -> account.withdraw(700, "Bob"), "Thread-Bob");

        t1.start();
        t2.start();

        t1.join();
        t2.join();

        System.out.println("Final Account Balance: ₹" + account.getBalance());
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Synchronization guarantees thread safety, mutual exclusion, and cash-in-register consistency.}$$
